WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.120
Elsewhere in this domain, we saw the keywords AND&nbsp;
and OR used for multiple criteria within a query.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.960
However, you may have multiple possibilities&nbsp;
for criteria you want within a field. For this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.720
instead of multiple OR conditions, you may want&nbsp;
to use an IN keyword. The IN keyword basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.640
means in one of these, as we are about to see.&nbsp;
Here is the same query we have used for several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.760
examples using the WHERE clause. In this example,&nbsp;
we are looking for people in one of three cities:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:38.800
Los Angeles, Long Beach, or Bellflower. I will add&nbsp;
a WHERE clause to this query and then add CITY IN&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.880
and, in parentheses, the three cities I mentioned&nbsp;
in single quotes and separated by commas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.360
I will execute this query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.720
and we see 380 rows, all from one of the three&nbsp;
cities in the criteria. Elsewhere in the domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.800
we saw the NOT keyword return the opposite of a&nbsp;
criterion. The same holds true for the IN keyword.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.840
If we want to see all the people outside of&nbsp;
these three cities, we can add the word NOT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.960
in front of the IN keyword&nbsp;
(NOT IN). When I execute this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:24.800
we see everyone who is not in Los&nbsp;
Angeles, Long Beach, or Bellflower.

